= Performance <sec-perf>

All timings are wall-clock medians over single operations on one machine (#bench-cpu, Windows,
#bench-rustc, release profile), measured by `cargo xtask bench` on #bench-date.slice(0, 10) on the
final code. They are not cycle counts, include frequency scaling and background load, and say
nothing about timing side channels. Suite rows and component rows are separate runs.

// Source: results/benchmarks.json (groups "kem-suite", "kem-component"), via paper/data/bench.typ
// (generated by paper/tools/gen_data.py). Medians and p95 in microseconds, n as listed in the file.
#figure(
  caption: [Hybrid KEM versus its components: medians in µs (n = 200 each); last column p95 of the
    hybrid. Component rows use VYRA's adapters with seed-form keys.],
  tsmall(table(
    columns: (auto, auto, auto, auto, auto, auto),
    align: (left, left, right, right, right, right),
    table.hline(stroke: 0.6pt),
    table.header([*Suite*], [*Op*], [*ML-KEM*], [*HQC*], [*Hybrid*], [*p95*]),
    table.hline(stroke: 0.4pt),
    ..(("VYRA-KEM-1", "ML-KEM-512", "HQC-128"), ("VYRA-KEM-3", "ML-KEM-768", "HQC-192"),
       ("VYRA-KEM-5", "ML-KEM-1024", "HQC-256")).map(((s, a, b)) =>
      ("keygen", "encaps", "decaps").map(op => (
        if op == "keygen" { s } else { [] }, op,
        med("kem-component", a, op), med("kem-component", b, op),
        med("kem-suite", s, op), p95("kem-suite", s, op),
      )).flatten()
    ).flatten(),
    table.hline(stroke: 0.6pt),
  )),
) <tab-bench-kem>

// Source: results/benchmarks.json (groups "kem-suite", "kem-component"), via paper/data/bench.typ.
#figure(
  caption: [Median times of the KEM components and the hybrid (µs, linear scale). HQC dominates
    every hybrid operation; the port has no hardware carry-less multiplication.],
  {
    let groups = (("1", "ML-KEM-512", "HQC-128", "VYRA-KEM-1"), ("3", "ML-KEM-768", "HQC-192",
      "VYRA-KEM-3"), ("5", "ML-KEM-1024", "HQC-256", "VYRA-KEM-5"))
    let ops = ("keygen", "encaps", "decaps")
    let vals = ()
    for (_, a, b, s) in groups {
      for op in ops {
        vals.push(float(med("kem-component", a, op)))
        vals.push(float(med("kem-component", b, op)))
        vals.push(float(med("kem-suite", s, op)))
      }
    }
    let vmax = calc.max(..vals)
    let W = 52mm
    let colors = (rgb("#7a9cc6"), rgb("#c9a227"), luma(60))
    let names = ("ML-KEM", "HQC", "hybrid")
    let cells = ()
    for (lvl, a, b, s) in groups {
      for op in ops {
        let vs = (med("kem-component", a, op), med("kem-component", b, op), med("kem-suite", s, op))
        cells.push(align(right + horizon, text(size: 6.4pt)[KEM-#lvl #op]))
        cells.push(stack(dir: ttb, spacing: 0.3mm, ..vs.enumerate().map(((i, v)) => stack(
          dir: ltr, spacing: 1mm,
          box(width: float(v) / vmax * W, height: 1.5mm, fill: colors.at(i)),
          text(size: 5.2pt, v),
        ))))
      }
    }
    set align(left)
    grid(columns: (17mm, 1fr), column-gutter: 1.5mm, row-gutter: 1.1mm, ..cells)
    v(0.6mm)
    text(size: 6.4pt)[
      #for (i, n) in names.enumerate() [
        #box(width: 3mm, height: 1.6mm, fill: colors.at(i)) #n #h(0.8em)
      ]
    ]
  },
) <fig-bench>

*The hybrid costs about the sum of its parts, and HQC dominates.* For VYRA-KEM-1, decapsulation takes
#med("kem-suite", "VYRA-KEM-1", "decaps") µs against #med("kem-component", "ML-KEM-512", "decaps")
µs for ML-KEM-512 and #med("kem-component", "HQC-128", "decaps") µs for HQC-128. Decapsulation
includes two full key generations, because secret keys are seeds and both public keys are
re-derived for the combiner. The port uses no hardware carry-less multiplication (@sec-conf).
Measurement noise is visible: the component run of HQC-256 decapsulation has a larger median
(#med("kem-component", "HQC-256", "decaps") µs, p95 #p95("kem-component", "HQC-256", "decaps") µs)
than the whole VYRA-KEM-5 decapsulation in the suite run (#med("kem-suite", "VYRA-KEM-5", "decaps")
µs), and VYRA-KEM-5 key generation has a median of #med("kem-suite", "VYRA-KEM-5", "keygen") µs
against a minimum of #row("kem-suite", "VYRA-KEM-5", "keygen").at(6) µs. Single numbers here should
be read to within tens of per cent. Sealing 1 KiB with VYRA-KEM-1 takes
#med("seal", "VYRA-KEM-1", "seal-1KiB") µs, close to one encapsulation.

// Source: results/benchmarks.json (groups "sig-suite", "sig-component"), via paper/data/bench.typ.
