    table.hline(stroke: 0.3pt),
    ..("SLH-DSA-SHAKE-128s", "SLH-DSA-SHAKE-192s", "SLH-DSA-SHAKE-256s").map(s => (
      s, us-to-ms(med("sig-component", s, "keygen")), us-to-ms(med("sig-component", s, "sign-1KiB")),
      us-to-ms(med("sig-component", s, "verify-1KiB")),
    )).flatten(),
    table.hline(stroke: 0.6pt),
  )),
) <tab-bench-sig>

// Source: paper/figures/perf-sig.svg, drawn by tools/figures/gen_figures.py from results/.
#figure(
  placement: bottom,
  scope: "parent",
  caption: [Signature medians on a logarithmic axis (the suite rows of @tab-bench-sig). Same sample sizes and caveats.],
  image("../figures/perf-sig.svg", width: 100%),
) <fig-bench-sig>

*Dual post-quantum signatures are slow and large.* Signing takes on the order of seconds (@fig-bench-sig;
#us-to-ms(med("sig-suite", "VYRA-SIG-PQ1", "sign-1KiB")) ms for VYRA-SIG-PQ1,
#us-to-ms(med("sig-suite", "VYRA-SIG-PQ3", "sign-1KiB")) ms for VYRA-SIG-PQ3), dominated by
SLH-DSA "s" signing, and signatures are 10--34 KB (@tab-suites). Composite signing additionally
recomputes `PK.root` to derive the public key for $M'$, a second SLH-DSA key generation per
signature; this is the price of seed-only keys. Transition signatures cost about a millisecond.
These costs, not the composition, limit where the dual post-quantum suites are usable.
